# Engineering core

## Precedence
company requirements > team standards (these rules) > project architecture > existing patterns > feature request.
If two of them conflict, name the conflict and ask. Never pick one silently.
"Existing pattern wins" means you follow the repo's conventions even when a generic best
practice differs.

## Reuse before create
- Before adding a component, service, hook, util, API, DTO, model or query, search for an
  existing one by name and by behaviour.
- Prefer extending an existing one over creating a parallel one.
- Never add a dependency for something a few lines, the stdlib, or an installed package
  already covers.

## Scope
- Change only what the approved plan names.
- If you discover a material change to scope or architecture, stop and use the STOP menu
  from AGENTS.md.
- Do not reformat, rename or "tidy" unrelated code.

## Code
- Optimise for the next reader: clear names, small functions, one responsibility per unit.
- No speculative abstraction. Do not add interfaces, factories, options or config without a
  second real use.
- Comments explain *why*, not *what*. Public contracts get doc comments in the language's
  idiom.
- Handle errors at the boundary where you can act on them. Never swallow an error, and never
  return a fake success.
- Log with the project's logger, at the right level, with context and without secrets.

## Tests
- New logic or a bug fix comes with a test that would have failed before the change.
- Never delete, skip or weaken a test, a lint rule or a type check to get green. Fix the code
  or raise the issue instead.

## Honesty
- Report failures as failures, and name any check you could not run.
- Do not claim something works unless you ran it and saw it work.
